@page "/blogs/add-cross-cutting-concerns-to-ef-core-without-touching-your-repositories"
@using BaseComponents
@inherits BasePage

<Content FileName=@nameof(EFCoreInterceptorsCrossCuttingConcerns)>
    <ContentBody>
        <What>
            <p>
                <Highlight>EF Core interceptors</Highlight> hook into selected points in the <Highlight>DbContext</Highlight> pipeline, including connection operations, query compilation, commands, and saves. This article shows how to use them for connection diagnostics, query limits, save-result logging, and basic audit events.
            </p>
            <p>
                An interceptor runs only for operations that pass through a configured EF Core context. It does not automatically observe direct ADO.NET calls or operations that bypass <Highlight>SaveChanges</Highlight>.
            </p>
        </What>

        <Why>
            <p>
                Repeating logging and audit setup in every repository makes behavior easy to apply inconsistently. A new code path can omit the shared logic even though it compiles and appears to work.
            </p>
            <p>
                Interceptors centralize behavior for EF Core operations, but they do not guarantee a complete audit history by themselves. The application still needs to decide which changes to record and account for raw SQL, bulk operations, and other paths that do not call <Highlight>SaveChanges</Highlight>.
            </p>
        </Why>

        <How>
            <h4>Where interceptors fit in the DbContext lifecycle</h4>
            <p>
                Interceptors attach to specific EF Core operations; they are not stages in one fixed <Highlight>DbContext</Highlight> lifecycle:
            </p>
            <CodeSnippet CssClass="language-text">
DbContext operations
  |-- Query compilation: IQueryExpressionInterceptor
  |-- Connection events: DbConnectionInterceptor
  |-- Commands: DbCommandInterceptor
  |-- Saves: SaveChangesInterceptor
  `-- Connection disposal: DbConnectionInterceptor
            </CodeSnippet>
            <p>
                Choose an interceptor lifetime based on its state. A stateless, thread-safe interceptor can be shared as a singleton; an interceptor that holds request- or context-specific state should use a scoped lifetime. Follow EF Core's lifetime requirements for interceptor interfaces that require singleton registration.
            </p>

            <h4>Log connection activity with a DbConnectionInterceptor</h4>
            <p>
                This example logs connection creation synchronously and closure and disposal asynchronously. Add the synchronous close and dispose callbacks too if the application uses synchronous database operations; override the opened callbacks to measure connection-open events.
            </p>
            <CodeSnippet CssClass="language-csharp">
public class OrderConnectionDiagnosticsInterceptor(
    ILogger&lt;OrderConnectionDiagnosticsInterceptor&gt; logger) : DbConnectionInterceptor
{
    public override DbConnection ConnectionCreated(ConnectionCreatedEventData eventData, DbConnection result)
    {
        logger.LogInformation("Connection created for {DataSource}", result.DataSource);
        return result;
    }

    public override async Task ConnectionClosedAsync(DbConnection connection, ConnectionEndEventData eventData)
    {
        logger.LogInformation("Connection closed after {Duration}", eventData.Duration);
        return await base.ConnectionClosedAsync(connection, eventData);
    }

    public override async Task ConnectionDisposedAsync(DbConnection connection, ConnectionEndEventData eventData)
    {
        logger.LogInformation("Connection disposed");
        return await base.ConnectionDisposedAsync(connection, eventData);
    }
}
            </CodeSnippet>
            <p>
                This interceptor holds no context-specific state, so it can be shared as a singleton if the logger and any added state remain thread-safe. Scoped registration is also appropriate when you need request-specific dependencies.
            </p>

            <h4>Cap unbounded result sets with a query expression interceptor</h4>
            <p>
                A query expression interceptor can add a limit before EF Core compiles a query. Applying the same limit to every query can also truncate reports or change pagination results, so use a global limit only when that behavior is valid for every affected query.
            </p>
            <CodeSnippet CssClass="language-csharp">
public class MaxRowLimitInterceptor : IQueryExpressionInterceptor
{
    private const int DefaultRowLimit = 200;

    private static readonly MethodInfo TakeMethodInfo = typeof(Queryable)
        .GetMethods()
        .Where(m => m.Name == nameof(Queryable.Take)
            &amp;&amp; m.GetParameters().Length == 2
            &amp;&amp; m.GetParameters()[1].ParameterType == typeof(int))
        .Single();

    public Expression QueryCompilationStarting(Expression queryExpression, QueryExpressionEventData eventData)
    {
        var entityType = queryExpression.Type.GetGenericArguments().FirstOrDefault();
        if (entityType is null)
        {
            return queryExpression;
        }

        var genericTakeMethod = TakeMethodInfo.MakeGenericMethod(entityType);
        return Expression.Call(genericTakeMethod, queryExpression, Expression.Constant(DefaultRowLimit));
    }
}
            </CodeSnippet>
            <p>
                This interceptor has no per-context state and can be registered as a singleton. Keep it thread-safe if multiple contexts use the same instance.
            </p>

            <h4>Log zero-entry saves with a SaveChangesInterceptor</h4>
            <p>
                <Highlight>SaveChangesAsync</Highlight> returns the number of state entries written. A zero result can be expected, so this interceptor reports it for review rather than treating it as an error.
            </p>
            <CodeSnippet CssClass="language-csharp">
public class NoOpSaveGuardInterceptor(ILogger&lt;NoOpSaveGuardInterceptor&gt; logger) : SaveChangesInterceptor
{
    public override async ValueTask&lt;int&gt; SavedChangesAsync(
        SaveChangesCompletedEventData eventData,
        int result,
        CancellationToken cancellationToken = default)
    {
        if (result &lt; 1)
        {
            logger.LogWarning("SaveChangesAsync completed but wrote zero state entries");
        }

        return await base.SavedChangesAsync(eventData, result, cancellationToken);
    }
}
            </CodeSnippet>

            <h4>Write audit rows automatically with the same interceptor type</h4>
            <p>
                A <Highlight>SaveChangesInterceptor</Highlight> can add audit entities from tracked changes before the save. This example records the entity type, operation, user name, and time; it does not record keys or property values and is not a complete audit history. In an ASP.NET Core application, use the request's user identity rather than <Highlight>Thread.CurrentPrincipal</Highlight>. Background jobs need an explicit actor identity.
            </p>
            <CodeSnippet CssClass="language-csharp">
public class AuditTrailInterceptor(IHttpContextAccessor httpContextAccessor) : SaveChangesInterceptor
{
    public override async ValueTask&lt;InterceptionResult&lt;int&gt;&gt; SavingChangesAsync(
        DbContextEventData eventData,
        InterceptionResult&lt;int&gt; result,
        CancellationToken cancellationToken = default)
    {
        if (eventData.Context is not null)
        {
            var auditEntries = BuildAuditEntries(eventData.Context.ChangeTracker);
            await eventData.Context.Set&lt;AuditEntry&gt;().AddRangeAsync(auditEntries, cancellationToken);
        }

        return await base.SavingChangesAsync(eventData, result, cancellationToken);
    }

    private List&lt;AuditEntry&gt; BuildAuditEntries(ChangeTracker changeTracker) =&gt;
        [.. changeTracker.Entries()
            .Where(entry =&gt; entry.State is EntityState.Added or EntityState.Modified or EntityState.Deleted)
            .Select(entry =&gt; new AuditEntry
            {
                EntityName = entry.Entity.GetType().Name,
                Action = entry.State.ToString(),
                ChangedBy = httpContextAccessor.HttpContext?.User.Identity?.Name ?? "Unknown",
                ChangedOnUtc = DateTime.UtcNow
            })];
}
            </CodeSnippet>
            <p>
                The audit entities join the context's pending changes and are saved with them. With EF Core's normal transactional save behavior, both sets of rows commit or roll back together.
            </p>

            <h4>Register every interceptor and get the lifetime right</h4>
            <p>
                Register the interceptors with the lifetimes their state requires, then pass them to <Highlight>AddInterceptors</Highlight> from the context configuration:
            </p>
            <CodeSnippet CssClass="language-csharp">
// Program.cs
builder.Services.AddScoped&lt;IInterceptor, OrderConnectionDiagnosticsInterceptor&gt;();
builder.Services.AddScoped&lt;IInterceptor, NoOpSaveGuardInterceptor&gt;();
builder.Services.AddScoped&lt;IInterceptor, AuditTrailInterceptor&gt;();
builder.Services.AddSingleton&lt;IInterceptor, MaxRowLimitInterceptor&gt;();
builder.Services.AddHttpContextAccessor();

// OrderContext.cs
public class OrderContext(
    DbContextOptions&lt;OrderContext&gt; options,
    IEnumerable&lt;IInterceptor&gt; interceptors) : DbContext(options)
{
    protected override void OnConfiguring(DbContextOptionsBuilder optionsBuilder) =&gt;
        optionsBuilder.AddInterceptors(interceptors);
}
            </CodeSnippet>
            <p>
                Every operation through this configured context uses these interceptors. Review the query limit, audit scope, and logging policy before applying the same configuration to every context.
            </p>
        </How>

        <Summary>
            <p>
                Key takeaways from this article:
            </p>
            <ul>
                <li><Highlight>Interceptors</Highlight> observe selected EF Core operations through configured contexts; they do not cover direct ADO.NET or every bulk-operation path</li>
                <li>A <Highlight>DbConnectionInterceptor</Highlight> gives you visibility into connection open/close/dispose events for diagnostics</li>
                <li>An <Highlight>IQueryExpressionInterceptor</Highlight> can rewrite a query's expression tree before it compiles - useful for guardrails like row limits</li>
                <li>A <Highlight>SaveChangesInterceptor</Highlight> can log save results or add audit entities, but a complete audit design needs keys, values, and coverage for non-<Highlight>SaveChanges</Highlight> operations</li>
                <li>Use singleton registration only for thread-safe interceptors whose state can be shared; otherwise scope their state appropriately</li>
            </ul>
            <p>
                Explore the <NavLink href="channels/efcore/">EF Core tutorial and learning path</NavLink> for the full Entity Framework Core series. What to read next: now that cross-cutting concerns are handled, learn how to <Highlight>enforce schema standards automatically</Highlight> in <NavLink href="blogs/enforce-database-schema-standards-automatically-with-ef-core-conventions/">EF Core conventions for Code First schema standards</NavLink>, or jump ahead to <NavLink href="blogs/catch-slow-queries-and-rewrite-them-on-the-fly-with-ef-core-command-interceptors/">EF Core command interceptors for slow SQL</NavLink> to see interception at the raw ADO.NET command layer.
            </p>
        </Summary>
    </ContentBody>
